This raises a fundamental limitation of how GIAs are typically evaluated. Prior work often reports reconstruction quality using reference-based metrics such as SSIM, PSNR, and LPIPS for image data, or BLEU and ROUGE for text data. These metrics require access to the corresponding ground-truth samples and, as noted by~\citeA{veroTabLeakTabularData2023}, do not reflect the attacker's perspective, since the adversary does not have access to the original batch and therefore cannot use them to verify the correctness of individual reconstructions. Moreover, such metrics may not always reflect the actual quality of the recovered information. A reconstruction can obtain a relatively high score while failing to faithfully represent the original input, potentially leading to an inaccurate assessment of the privacy risk posed by the attack. Figure~\ref{fig:scale_mia_rec} illustrates this limitation through reconstructions obtained through the attack proposed by~\cite{shiScaleMIAScalableModel2025} on CIFAR10 data. In the examples shown, representations containing information from multiple samples can achieve relatively high PSNR values, which, according to the reconstruction success criterion adopted in their work, would be classified as correctly recovered samples, despite failing to recover any recognizable input. Consequently, reference-based metrics alone may provide an incomplete assessment of reconstruction success and of the information actually recovered by the attacker.


Human inspection can sometimes help identify correct reconstructions in image or text data, but it must be performed candidate by candidate and within the limited time window of the attack, making it a costly bottleneck for large batches. For tabular records, manual validation is even more difficult, since numerical values do not provide comparable semantic cues. In particular, a convex combination of continuous features may appear indistinguishable from an actual training sample without access to the original data (Figure~\ref{fig:verification}). The inability to identify genuine recoveries therefore has important implications for the practical privacy risk posed by reconstruction attacks. Even an attack that consistently recovers a small fraction of samples, e.g., $10\%$, can represent a serious privacy threat. However, if the attacker cannot distinguish correctly recovered records from spurious candidates, the reported reconstruction rate may overestimate the amount of private information that the adversary can reliably identify and exploit.


Ideally, GIAs should therefore provide an intrinsic verification mechanism. Despite its practical importance, verifiability has been largely overlooked in the GIA literature; only a few works, mostly in the honest-but-curious setting, offer any form of verification, typically under restrictive conditions.

\citeA{veroTabLeakTabularData2023} introduce an entropy-based verification score, but computing it requires solving the same optimization problem multiple times, resulting in a costly heuristic rather than a formal guarantee. Analytical attacks from ~\citeA{dimitrovSPEARExactGradient2024} and \citeA{bakarsky2025spearscalinggradientinversion} provide intrinsic verification, but require the batch size to be smaller than the input dimension—a restrictive condition for tabular data. Moreover, these verification mechanisms are tied to honest-but-curious attacks, whose reconstruction performance heavily degrades in large-batch regimes. In the malicious setting, the Cutting Thtough Privacy attack developed in Chapter~\ref{chap:ctp} prescribed tolerance and
cannot intrinsically determine whether a sufficiently narrow non-empty
region contains one record or several nearby records. Its success criterion therefore depends on an unknown separation between client samples and does not provide a per-reconstruction certificate. (see Section~\ref{subsec:preliminaries}).

\section{A Verifiable Gradient Inversion Attack}
\subsection{Threat Model}\label{sec:vgia_threat_model}
Our attack considers the same malicious server setting described in Section~\ref{sec:threat}, consistent with the CTP attack setting. The server is not allowed to modify the model architecture, for example by adding layers or modules~\cite{fowlRobbingFedDirectly2021,fowlDecepticonsCorruptedTransformers2022,zhaoLOKILargescaleData2023a,shiScaleMIAScalableModel2025}, changing internal connections or routing mechanisms~\cite{fowlDecepticonsCorruptedTransformers2022,chu2023panning}, or altering activation functions~\cite{fowlRobbingFedDirectly2021}. Second, we do not assume access to auxiliary or public datasets, unlike~\cite{boenischWhenCuriousAbandon2023,ZHANG2023119421,garovHidingPlainSight2024,shiScaleMIAScalableModel2025}, nor auxiliary knowledge of the data distribution beyond known bounds on the input features, unlike~\cite{fowlRobbingFedDirectly2021,fowlDecepticonsCorruptedTransformers2022,shiScaleMIAScalableModel2025}. In the following, we focus on clients updating the global model with full-batch FedSGD. We also provide empirical results for FedAvg in Section~\ref{sec:vgia_fedavg}.

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{subsec:preliminaries}
Our work builds upon the fundamental observation that the gradients of a fully connected (FC) layer followed by a ReLU activation can contain sufficient information to analytically recover input data, described in Section~\ref{sec:ctp_background}. Consider a model whose first (attacked) layer is a linear layer with $N$ neurons, parameterized by weights $\W^a \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}$ and biases $\b^a \in \mathbb{R}^N$. For neuron $i$, the post-activation output is
\[
z_i^a = \mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(\W_i^a \x + b_i^a\right),
\]
where $\x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ denotes the input to the attacked layer. As shown in Equation~\ref{eq:aggr_obs}, the attacker can retrieve a linear combination of the batch inputs, where only samples that activate neuron $i$ have a nonzero coefficient $\alpha_j$, and therefore contribute to $\o_i$. Disentangling individual samples from the aggregate $\o_i$ is the central challenge in analytic gradient inversion.

To address this limitations of existing reconstruction attacks, the previous chapter introduced a geometric view of ReLU-based leakage, detailed in Section~\ref{sec:limit}, which led to the CTP attack presented in Section~\ref{sec:our_attack}. By interpreting ReLU activation boundaries as hyperplanes, CTP searches for regions containing individual inputs and reconstructs them from the corresponding gradient contributions.

CTP can identify empty slices and avoid querying them further, but it cannot determine whether a non-empty slice contains one sample or several. Consequently, the algorithm continues bisecting every non-empty slice until its width falls below a user-chosen threshold $\varepsilon$. The choice of $\varepsilon$ introduces two limitations. First, if two inputs have projections along the normal direction of $\W_i^a$ that differ by less than $\varepsilon$, CTP may stop before separating them and return an incorrect reconstruction. Second, choosing $\varepsilon$ too small may lead to unnecessary rounds spent refining slices that already contain a single sample. Figure~\ref{fig:ctp_ok} illustrates a successful case, where CTP recovers three inputs after 6 rounds, using 3 hyperplanes per round and 18 hyperplanes in total. In contrast, Figure~\ref{fig:ctp_miss} shows a failure case in which two inputs remain in the same $\varepsilon$-wide slice. In a realistic blind threat model, the adversary does not know the relevant sample separations in advance and therefore cannot reliably tune $\varepsilon$.


